Colombo, Sep. 24 (Daily Mirror) - Former Krrish Group Director Janaki Siriwardena has been arrested by the Commission to Investigate Allegations of Bribery or Corruption (CIABOC).
The Supreme Court’s determination on the 22nd Amendment was communicated to Parliament and announced by Speaker Dr. Jagath Wickramaratne on Tuesday (22).
The Bill can therefore proceed through Parliament subject to the constitutional requirement applicable to a constitutional amendment.
Parliament has scheduled the second reading debate on the 22nd Amendment and the Judicature (Amendment) Bill for Thursday (24) and Friday (25).
The determination was made by a five-member Supreme Court Bench comprising Chief Justice Preethi Padman Surasena and Justices Achala Wengappuli, Arjuna Obeyesekere, Sampath Abayakoon and Gihan Kulatunga.
The proposed 22nd Amendment contains several provisions that would alter the retirement ages of judges of the superior courts and increase the maximum number of judges of the Court of Appeal.
Here are the main provisions of the proposed amendment and the separate Judicature (Amendment) Bill.
The main provisions of 22nd Amendment to the Constitution 1.
Increase the retirement age of Supreme Court judges to 67 years from 65.
Increase the retirement age of Court of Appeal judges to 65 years from 63.
Chief Justice would retire upon reaching 67 years or upon completion of six years from the date of appointment.
Six-year limitation on the term of office of Chief Justice would not apply to incumbent Chief Justice.
Increase the maximum number of other judges of the Court of Appeal from 19 to 24.
The Supreme Court also determined that the provisions of the Judicature (Amendment) Bill are not inconsistent with the Constitution and can be passed by the simple majority of Parliament.
The main provisions of the Judicature (Amendment) Bill 1.
Increase the number of Judges of the High Court from 110 to 120.
Increase the retirement age of High Court judges to 63 years from 61.
Increase the retirement age of other judges and Magistrates to 62 years.
INDIA TODAY - Fifteen people convicted over Sri Lanka’s 2019 Easter Sunday suicide attacks will challenge their jail terms of more than 200 years each, a defence lawyer said on Wednesday.
Nearly 270 people, including 11 Indians, were killed in the bombings and more than 500 were injured.
On Tuesday, a three-judge High Court Trial-at-Bar found 15 of the 24 accused guilty of involvement in the attacks and handed each of them prison terms of more than 200 years.
The bench comprised Judges Navaratne Marasinghe, Ramanathan Kannan and Sujeewa Nissanka.
Defence counsel Kneel Maddumage, who appeared for seven of the accused, said the convictions and the record jail terms would be challenged in the Supreme Court after the relevant documents are received.
“Some of the defendants are not represented by counsel, but I requested on behalf of all the convicts that they be provided with copies of the proceedings to file their appeals,” Maddumage said.
Initially, 25 people were charged over their role in the bombings.
After one of the accused died, 24 were indicted in October 2021.
A total of 23,270 charges were brought against them, and 2,309 witnesses testified for the prosecution.
Catholic Church spokesman Father Jude Krishantha welcomed the verdict and said it had brought a sense of justice to the victims.
“We feel satisfied because the relatives of those who died were expecting justice,” Krishantha said.
In July, former defence ministry bureaucrat Hemasiri Fernando and former police chief Pujith Jayasundara were sentenced to death for failing to act on prior intelligence that could have prevented the attacks.
The bombings had triggered a political storm, with then President Maithripala Sirisena and then Prime Minister Ranil Wickremesinghe blamed for failing to prevent them despite prior intelligence being available.
A presidential panel appointed by Sirisena after the attacks later found the then president guilty of failing to prevent them, though Sirisena pleaded not guilty in the case filed after the panel’s findings.
The High Court Trial-at-Bar comprising Justices Navaratne Marasinghe, Ramanathan Kannan and Sujeewa Nishshanka delivered the verdict.
Furthermore, the properties of all 15 convicted accused were ordered to be confiscated.
The verdict was delivered as the final judgement in the trial relating to the Easter Sunday terror attacks, which claimed the lives of 269 people and injured more than 500 others.
However, the court acquitted and released nine other accused who had also been charged in connection with the attacks.
The terrorist attacks, targeting three Catholic churches in Sri Lanka and three leading hotels in Colombo, took place on Easter Sunday, April 21, 2019.
The series of attacks was carried out by nine suicide bombers, including National Thawheed Jamaath leader Zahran Hashim.
The attacks claimed the lives of 269 people, including Sri Lankans, while more than 500 others, including children, were injured, drawing international attention.
Following investigations, legal proceedings were instituted in 2019 against 24 accused over more than 23,270 charges, including conspiring to carry out the Easter Sunday attacks and providing assistance and abetment.
A total of 2,309 prosecution witnesses had given evidence in the case filed by the Attorney General against the 24 accused, including Naufer Moulavi.
The delivery of the judgement commenced before the three-member Trial-at-Bar at around 12.30 p.m.
Delivering the judgement, President of the Trial-at-Bar, High Court Judge Navaratne Marasinghe, stated that only the most important matters of the very lengthy judgement would be read out in open court.
Accordingly, the judge stated that an indictment comprising nine volumes and 8,900 pages had been served on the accused.
Accordingly, the court considered the matters presented against the accused regarding the conducting of workshops and maintaining safe houses for extremist lectures and weapons training prior to the Easter Sunday attacks.
A conspiracy and an agreement can take place either directly or impliedly.
Those who became involved at the beginning, those who became involved in the middle, as well as those who became involved at the end, can all be parties to the conspiracy.”
Thereafter, the judge stated that the defence pleas presented by each of the accused had been considered before the court.
Accordingly, the court rejected the defence pleas presented by 15 accused.
However, the High Court Judge stated that the court accepted the defence pleas presented by the other nine accused.
The remaining nine accused were acquitted and released.
